---
layout: base.njk
title: Mindchord
nav: home
---

{% block head %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/index_style.css">
{% endblock %}

<!-- First screen: hero + EEG canvas. On mobile this wrapper is a full-height
     flex column (`100svh − header`) so the hero fills the viewport the way it
     does on desktop. `md:contents` dissolves the wrapper at ≥768px, leaving the
     original desktop flow (section then canvas, normal block flow) untouched. -->
<div class="md:contents flex flex-col min-h-[calc(100svh-4rem)]">

<!-- Hero -->
<section class="px-6 md:px-24 pt-12 md:pt-24 pb-6 md:pb-10 relative z-10 flex-1 flex flex-col justify-center md:block">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
        <h1 class="wordmark text-[52px] md:text-[64px] leading-[1.05] tracking-tight">
            Upgrade your mind.
        </h1>
        <p class="wordmark mt-5 md:mt-6 text-lg md:text-xl text-[#6B635A] leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
            Mindchord is a take-home neurofeedback app that connects to portable EEG headsets to complete sessions set by providers.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 md:mt-10 flex justify-center">
            <a href="#about"
                class="relative isolate inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-8 py-4 text-[16px] text-[#F5F0EB] wordmark rounded-full ring-4 ring-transparent hover:ring-[#7A8F7E]/30 active:scale-95 transition-all duration-200 before:content-[''] before:absolute before:inset-0 before:-z-10 before:rounded-full before:bg-gradient-to-b before:from-[#869C8A] before:to-[#6A7F6E] before:transition-all before:duration-200 hover:before:brightness-90">
                Find out more
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="w-4 h-4">
                    <path d="M5.3 10.8a1 1 0 0 1 0-1.4 1 1 0 0 1 1.42 0L12 14.68l5.28-5.28a1 1 0 1 1 1.42 1.42l-5.83 5.83a1.25 1.25 0 0 1-1.77 0L5.3 10.8Z"/>
                </svg>
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- EEG visualisation canvas. Mobile: a taller band anchored at the bottom of
     the full-height first screen (no overlap). Desktop: pulled up under the
     hero as before via the negative margin. -->
<div class="w-full mt-0 md:-mt-24 relative z-0">
    <canvas id="eeg-canvas" class="block w-full eeg-canvas-h"></canvas>
</div>

</div><!-- /first screen -->

<!-- About -->
<!-- Layout: sticky left rail TOC + right content column. Subsections are -->
<!-- anchored so the rail can scroll-spy and clicks deep-link cleanly. -->
<section class="px-6 md:px-24 pt-12 md:pt-40 pb-20 md:pb-32">
    <h2 id="about" class="wordmark text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight scroll-mt-28">About</h2>

    <!-- Mobile-only About nav: sticky horizontal scroll-spy chips. The desktop
         vertical rail (below, `hidden md:block`) is hidden on mobile, so this
         gives small screens a sense of place + quick jumps. Shares scroll-spy. -->
    <nav id="about-toc-mobile" class="md:hidden sticky top-16 z-30 -mx-6 px-6 mt-5 mb-2 py-2.5 glass overflow-x-auto no-scrollbar">
        <ul class="flex items-center gap-2 w-max wordmark text-[14px]">
            <li><a href="#about-neurofeedback" class="toc-chip" data-target="about-neurofeedback">Neurofeedback</a></li>
            <li><a href="#about-mindchord" class="toc-chip" data-target="about-mindchord">What is Mindchord?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#about-app" class="toc-chip" data-target="about-app">The App</a></li>
            <li><a href="#about-feedback" class="toc-chip" data-target="about-feedback">Technical info</a></li>
            <li><a href="#about-console" class="toc-chip" data-target="about-console">Console</a></li>
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="mt-8 md:mt-16 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[200px_1fr] gap-10 md:gap-16">
        <!-- Sticky table of contents -->
        <aside class="hidden md:block">
            <nav id="about-toc" class="sticky top-24 wordmark text-[15px]">
                <ul class="space-y-3 border-l border-[#6B635A]/20 pl-5">
                    <li><a href="#about-neurofeedback" class="toc-link" data-target="about-neurofeedback">What is neurofeedback?</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#about-mindchord" class="toc-link" data-target="about-mindchord">What is Mindchord?</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#about-app" class="toc-link" data-target="about-app">The App - Images</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#about-feedback" class="toc-link" data-target="about-feedback">Technical feedback info</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#about-console" class="toc-link" data-target="about-console">Provider Console</a></li>
                </ul>
            </nav>
        </aside>

        <!-- Right column: subsection content. Narrow column = comfortable read line length. -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-24">

            <!-- What is neurofeedback? -->
            <article id="about-neurofeedback" class="about-subsection scroll-mt-28">
                <h3 class="wordmark text-3xl tracking-tight">What is neurofeedback?</h3>
                <div class="wordmark mt-6 space-y-5 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-[#2C2825]/85">
                    <p>
                        Neurofeedback is a form of biofeedback that uses real-time measurements of brain activity — in our case EEG — to help a person learn to shift their own brain state. Sensors placed on the scalp pick up the electrical activity of the cortex underneath. That activity is processed into something the person can perceive: a sound that gets louder, a video that plays more clearly, a visual that brightens when the activity moves in a target direction.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The mechanism is operant conditioning. When the brain produces more of the desired pattern — say, a steadier rhythm in a certain frequency band — the feedback rewards it. Over time, that pattern becomes easier to produce. The person isn't told <em>how</em> to change their brain activity, and they usually can't describe what they're doing; the learning happens below the level of conscious strategy.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <!-- Mindchord -->
            <article id="about-mindchord" class="about-subsection scroll-mt-28">
                <h3 class="wordmark text-3xl tracking-tight">What is Mindchord?</h3>
                <div class="wordmark mt-6 space-y-5 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-[#2C2825]/85">
                    <p>
                        Mindchord is a lightweight neurofeedback app designed to be used by clients to complete neurofeedback sessions set by providers. The app comes in two parts: The neurofeedback app, which clients take home to complete sessions using portable EEG headsets, and the Provider Console, which providers use to set protocols and monitor clients. Providers set protocols based on what the client wants to address and any readings or examinations they perform (see <a href="#about-feedback" class="text-[#C4704B] underline decoration-[#C4704B]/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-[#C4704B] transition">Technical Feedback Info</a> for more details about what settings can be configured by providers).
                        The neurofeedback app offeres a variety of feedback types, including preset video feedback, local video feedback, and interactive visualisations.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Mindchord is still in closed beta, so updates are being made regularly. If you are interested in joining as a client or provider, please <a href="/contact" class="text-[#C4704B] underline decoration-[#C4704B]/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-[#C4704B] transition">reach out to us</a> - we would love to answer any questions you have or add you as a beta-tester.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <!-- The App — screenshot gallery. Each thumbnail is a button that opens
                    the shared lightbox (see #lightbox markup + lightbox() in
                    index_script.js). Landscape desktop shots, so 1-up on mobile, 2-up ≥640px. -->
            <article id="about-app" class="about-subsection scroll-mt-28">
                <h3 class="wordmark text-3xl tracking-tight">The App - Images</h3>

                <div class="gallery mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    {% for n in range(1, 7) %}
                    <button type="button" class="gallery-item">
                        <img src="/media/app-screenshot-{{ n }}.jpg" alt="Mindchord app screenshot {{ n }}" loading="lazy" class="w-full h-auto block">
                    </button>
                    {% endfor %}
                </div>
            </article>

            <!-- Technical feedback info -->
            <article id="about-feedback" class="about-subsection scroll-mt-28">
                <h3 class="wordmark text-3xl tracking-tight">Technical feedback info</h3>
                <div class="wordmark mt-6 space-y-5 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-[#2C2825]/85">
                    <p>
                        Each session begins with a short calibration period in which Mindchord records the brain's natural activity in the target frequency bands to establish a personal baseline. Once calibrated, the app continuously measures your live brainwave power, (using Welch's method to estimate the spectral density of the EEG signal, with artifact rejection to discard moments contaminated by blinks, jaw movement, or electrode noise), and compares it against the user's baseline as a z-score (how many standard deviations above or below their resting state they are right now).
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Feedback runs throughout the session, reflecting whether the user is currently above or below the protocol's threshold: the longer they stay above, the more the feedback intensifies, and the longer they stay below, the more it fades — reinforcing sustained self-regulation rather than fleeting peaks.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Protocols can combine up to two bands at once (for example, increasing one frequency while decreasing another), and clinicians can configure which electrode drives the feedback — any of the Muse headband's standard channels (TP9, AF7, AF8, TP10) or the optional AUX electrode plugged into the headband — along with an optional reference channel for software re-referencing to better isolate the region of interest.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </article>

            <!-- Clinician Console -->
            <article id="about-console" class="about-subsection scroll-mt-28">
                <h3 class="wordmark text-3xl tracking-tight">Provider Console</h3>
                <p class="wordmark mt-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-[#2C2825]/85">
                    The Mindchord Provider Console is the web companion to the desktop app. Providers use it to manage their clients, design and assign session protocols, and review session-level data — adherence, time-in-zone, signal quality, and streaks — as it flows back from each at-home session.
                </p>

                <!-- Screenshot gallery — landscape console captures, opens the shared lightbox. -->
                <div class="gallery mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    {% for n in range(1, 3) %}
                    <button type="button" class="gallery-item">
                        <img src="/media/console-screenshot-{{ n }}.jpg" alt="Mindchord Provider Console screenshot {{ n }}" loading="lazy" class="w-full h-auto block">
                    </button>
                    {% endfor %}
                </div>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Contact CTA — warm tinted band, single soft blob, single primary action -->
<section id="contact" class="relative overflow-hidden bg-[#EAE3DA] px-6 md:px-24 py-24 md:py-32 scroll-mt-28">
    <!-- Soft warm blob for atmosphere -->
    <div class="cta-blob"></div>

    <div class="relative max-w-2xl mx-auto text-center">
        <h2 class="wordmark text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight">Get in touch</h2>
        <p class="wordmark mt-5 text-lg md:text-xl text-[#6B635A] leading-relaxed">
            Mindchord is in beta. Whether you're a clinic looking to offer it to your clients, a clinician evaluating it for your practice, or someone interested in trying neurofeedback yourself, we'd love to hear from you.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-9 flex justify-center">
            <a href="/contact"
                class="relative isolate inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-7 py-3 text-[15px] text-[#F5F0EB] wordmark rounded-full ring-4 ring-transparent hover:ring-[#7A8F7E]/30 active:scale-95 transition-all duration-200 before:content-[''] before:absolute before:inset-0 before:-z-10 before:rounded-full before:bg-gradient-to-b before:from-[#869C8A] before:to-[#6A7F6E] before:transition-all before:duration-200 hover:before:brightness-90">
                Reach out
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="w-4 h-4">
                    <path d="M13.2 5.3a1 1 0 0 1 1.4 0l5.8 5.8a1.25 1.25 0 0 1 0 1.77l-5.8 5.83a1 1 0 0 1-1.42-1.41L17.18 13H4.75a1 1 0 1 1 0-2h12.43l-3.98-3.98a1 1 0 0 1 0-1.42Z"/>
                </svg>
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Lightbox overlay — shared by the App gallery. Hidden until a thumbnail is
     clicked; populated + driven by lightbox() in index_script.js. Works on
     desktop (side arrows, keyboard, click-outside) and mobile (swipe, tap-out). -->
<div id="lightbox" class="lightbox" aria-hidden="true" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="App screenshot viewer">
    <button type="button" class="lightbox-close" aria-label="Close viewer">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round">
            <path d="M6 6l12 12M18 6L6 18"/>
        </svg>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="lightbox-nav lightbox-prev" aria-label="Previous image">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7"/>
        </svg>
    </button>
    <figure class="lightbox-stage">
        <img class="lightbox-img" src="" alt="">
    </figure>
    <button type="button" class="lightbox-nav lightbox-next" aria-label="Next image">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/>
        </svg>
    </button>
</div>

<script src="/scripts/index_script.js"></script>